1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,560
If your nose turns unusually pale or you feel sharp, worsening pain after a filler injection, pay attention right away.

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:11,760
This isn't ordinary swelling — it can be an early sign that a blood vessel has been blocked.

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:20,480
Nose vessels sit close to the artery that supplies your eye, so when filler blocks one of them, blood flow stops and the tissue starts losing oxygen.

4
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,040
Here are three warning signs worth knowing.

5
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,760
First, pain that doesn't match the procedure.

6
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:31,040
Normal bruising eases over time, but pain from a blocked vessel keeps getting worse.

7
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,440
Second, changing skin color.

8
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:41,920
The area turns pale first, then within hours shifts to a mottled, purplish tone — a slow change easy to mistake for a bruise.

9
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:45,120
Third, and most critical, is the golden window.

10
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:51,600
Tissue can only survive without oxygen for a few hours, so waiting even a day can make recovery far harder.

11
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:57,760
If the area stays pale and the pain won't ease, contact your provider or go to urgent care immediately.

12
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:06,640
Before getting filler, ask whether the clinic uses ultrasound guidance and keeps hyaluronidase on hand for emergencies — that's the clearest way to stay safe.
